  "releaseNotes": "Breaking\n- ICached<T>.InstanceAsync() and GetCacheAsync() return ValueTask instead of Task; use .AsTask() where a Task is required (Task.WhenAll, AsyncContext.Run).\n- Methods returning a non-generic Task or ValueTask are no longer intercepted (they used to break the proxy); they always run.\n- Cache keys of string parameters changed ('_' and '\\' are escaped, null and \"\" are different keys), so file cache entries of such methods written by older versions are not hit and get filled again.\n\nPerformance\n- Cache hit without locks: async hit 15.8 us -> under 1 us; now ~160 ns with parameters (64 B), ~95 ns without (0 B).\n- The generated proxy calls the cache directly, with no reflection or hash lookup per call.\n- Expiration is scheduled once per entry instead of a queued job on every hit.\n- A key without parameters is computed once; key hashing does not allocate (same Murmur3 hash, same file names).\n- Clearing by type uses an index (10,000 entries: ~1 ms -> ~5 us).\n- Memory per entry ~1,100 B -> ~680 B (lock released after filling, entries keyed by a 128-bit number).\n\nFixes\n- A method that throws is not executed twice; callers waiting for the same entry get the same exception instead of calling the method one by one (cancellation and ObjectDisposedException are not shared).\n- ValueTask<T> methods are supported.\n- Expiration uses UTC (no DST jumps).\n- Clearing by an interface method clears only that method.\n- A Trace warning, once per type, when a cache key parameter has no GetHashCode of its own (the cache would never hit).",
